# Terms that must never appear in anything this package ships.
#
# Copy to `.identity-denylist` (gitignored) and fill in for your situation:
#
#     cp .identity-denylist.example .identity-denylist
#
# One term per line. Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. Matching is
# case-sensitive, so list each casing you care about separately — a codebase
# named `my_app` also appears as `MyApp` in module names and `MY_APP` in
# environment variables.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS LOCAL AND NOT COMMITTED
#
# The check guards against *authoring* leaks: content pasted in from the
# codebase this package was extracted from, carrying that codebase's name or
# domain vocabulary with it. That only ever happens on a maintainer's machine.
# A consumer who clones this package has nothing to leak, so the list would be
# noise to them — and committing it would publish the very names the check
# exists to keep out of the package.
#
# The consequence: on a clone without this file the check cannot run. `test.sh`
# reports it as **skipped**, never as passed, so a vacuous run is never
# mistaken for a clean one.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE
#
# Two categories, both from the codebase this was extracted from:
#
#   1. Its name, in every casing that appears in real text.
#   2. Its domain vocabulary — the nouns that mean something specific there and
#      nothing to anyone else. These leak more easily than the name, because
#      they read as ordinary words while you are writing.
#
# Do not add generic words. `workflow`, `backlog`, and `active` are this
# package's own vocabulary; listing them would fail the check on every file.

# --- name ---
my_app
MyApp
MY_APP

# --- domain vocabulary ---
SomeDomainNoun
AnotherDomainNoun
